1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A program is a file on disk. A process is that file being executed.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Run one program twice and you have two processes with two separate address spaces.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
So a process is three things: an address space, an execution context, and a record of it.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
The execution context lives in registers while running, and in the PCB while not.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Five states, and only some of the edges between them exist.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Ready means waiting for the CPU. Blocked means waiting for an event, not for the CPU.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
The edge that does not exist is blocked to running: a woken process joins the ready queue first.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Ready to blocked is also missing, because only a running process can ask for something.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
A context switch is a save and a load, and no user instruction retires during either.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
What is saved is the execution context: program counter, stack pointer, general registers.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
A process switch also changes the address space, so the translation cache is invalidated.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
With a 10 ms quantum and a 0.1 ms switch, 0.99 per cent of the time is spent switching.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
fork makes a copy of the calling process, and both copies return from the same call.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
The child receives 0. The parent receives the child's process identifier.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Each copy has its own memory, so a write in one is invisible in the other.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
But both copies print the same address, because the copy keeps the same virtual layout.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Three sequential forks: every process that exists runs the next fork.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
The count doubles each time: one, two, four, eight. So seven children and eight processes.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
A fork inside a loop that also waits counts prints, not processes: two, four, eight, total 14.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
So draw the tree and count the nodes. Never guess the power.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
A thread is a second execution context inside one process.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Shared: code, global data, heap, open files and the address space itself.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Private: program counter, registers, stack, and the thread's own identifier and state.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
So threads communicate for free and must synchronise; processes are isolated and must use IPC.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
A user-level thread is created and scheduled by a library; the kernel sees one process.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
If one user-level thread blocks in a system call, the kernel blocks the whole process.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Kernel threads are scheduled by the kernel, so one blocking thread does not stop the others.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Kernel threads of one process still share its code, so the 2014 statement is the false one.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Shared memory: the kernel maps one region into both address spaces, once.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Because nothing is copied, the processes themselves must agree when a write is finished.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Message passing: no shared region. Send and receive copy through the kernel.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Message passing can block instead of synchronise, and its buffer size is the parameter that matters.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
A pipe is a one-way byte stream between two processes, buffered by the kernel.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
A pipe preserves bytes, not messages: two writes can be read as one read.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
A named pipe has a name in the file system, so unrelated processes can open it.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Signals carry no data; sockets cross machine boundaries; message queues keep boundaries.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step one: is the question about states, about copies, or about sharing?

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step two: for fork draw the tree, for threads split the columns, for states check the edge list.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
The traps: blocked to running, children against processes, and a pipe treated as a message channel.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
One question under all of it: what does this process own, and what does it share?

